%[text] %[text:anchor:T_0B83894C] # Design and Analyze a Non-Isolated DC-DC Converter
%[text] A non-isolated DC-DC converter steps a DC voltage down (buck), up (boost), or both (buck-boost), each with a synchronous variant. This design solution shows how to size the power stage and select the semiconductors. The model uses the non-isolated DC-DC plant and controller blocks from `ConverterLibrary`. You then simulate the closed loop, plot the voltage and current (IV) waveforms, and estimate power loss and efficiency at a specified junction temperature.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A6556DB6] ## Design Solution Scope
%[text] In this design solution, you:
%[text] - Size the power stage and review the estimated design parameters
%[text] - Select semiconductors that meet the required ratings
%[text] - Simulate and inspect the input/output voltage and current (IV) waveforms
%[text] - Estimate power loss and efficiency at a specified device junction temperature \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B6482FCD] ## System Overview
%[text] The model has the Non-Isolated DCDC plant block and the Non-Isolated DCDC Controller. The controller compares the reference (`Vref`) with the measured output voltage and produces the PWM gate.![](text:image:132d)
%[text] ![](text:image:NonIsolatedDCDCModel.png)
modelName = "NonIsolatedDCDCConverter";
open_system(modelName);
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_design] ## Select Non-Isolated DC-DC Converter Parameters
%[text] To size the power stage, use the `EstimateNonIsolatedDCDCParameters` live script. You specify the topology and the design requirements. It returns the inductance, the output capacitance, the required device rating and the cascaded controller gains.
%[text] To open the parameter estimation workflow, enter this command in the MATLAB® Command Window:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] edit 'EstimateNonIsolatedDCDCParameters'
%[text] ```
%[text] Load the default parameters.
if ~exist('nonIsolatedDCDCDesign','var')
    NonIsolatedDCDCDefaultInputParameters;
end
disp(table(designTable,'VariableNames',"Non-Isolated DC-DC Estimated Design Parameters")); %[output:d1a7b3c9]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_selectcomp] ## Select Converter Components
%[text] This design solution helps you select the voltage and current ratings of the switch and the diode, and then catalog parts that meet those ratings. The parts come from the Simscape™ parts feature.
%[text] To open the component selection workflow, run:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] edit 'SelectNonIsolatedDCDCConverterComponents'
%[text] ```
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_op] ## Define the Operating Point
%[text] Set the input voltage, the output voltage and the load power for the waveform and loss analyses.
inputDCVoltage   = nonIsolatedDCDCDesign.inputVoltage;   % Input DC voltage (Vin)
voltageReference = nonIsolatedDCDCDesign.outputVoltage;  % Output voltage reference (Vref)
outputLoadPower  = nonIsolatedDCDCDesign.ratedPower;     % Output power (Pload)
simulationTime   = simscape.Value(20e-3, "s");           % Stop time, covers the 4 ms soft start
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_plot] ### Plot Converter Voltage and Current (IV Curves)
%[text] To plot the converter waveforms, use `getConverterWaveforms`. It sets the plant to fast ideal fidelity, simulates at the operating point, and plots input/output voltage (`Vin`, `Vout`), input/output current (`Iin`, `Iout`) and the inductor current (`IL`).
dcdcIVData = getConverterWaveforms(modelName, Converter="NonIsolatedDCDC", ... %[output:group:75e17871] %[output:5a8f32e2]
    Vref=voltageReference, Vin=inputDCVoltage, Pload=outputLoadPower, ... %[output:5a8f32e2]
    SimTime=simulationTime, FrequencyHz=switchingFrequency.value, FocusCycles=8); %[output:group:75e17871] %[output:5a8f32e2]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_loss] ### Estimate Power Loss and Efficiency
%[text] To estimate the switching and conduction losses and the efficiency, use `getConverterPowerLoss`. It simulates the plant's `Detailed` variant at the specified junction temperature.
simulationTemperature = simscape.Value(25,"degC");   % Device junction temperature for the loss estimation
lossData = getConverterPowerLoss(modelName, Converter="NonIsolatedDCDC", ... %[output:group:7c1d2732] %[output:83e6e0aa] %[output:8ce4da78]
    Vref=voltageReference, Vin=inputDCVoltage, Pload=outputLoadPower, ... %[output:83e6e0aa] %[output:8ce4da78]
    SimTime=simulationTime, SwitchingLossDetail="lumped", ... %[output:83e6e0aa] %[output:8ce4da78]
    SimulationTemperature=simulationTemperature); %[output:group:7c1d2732] %[output:83e6e0aa] %[output:8ce4da78]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_concl] ## Conclusion
%[text] This design solution shows how to use MATLAB scripting and Simscape Electrical™ to design a non-isolated DC-DC converter. It sizes the power stage and selects the semiconductors, models the circuit at low fidelity for fast simulation and control design, and models it in enough detail to predict losses and efficiency.
